WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.465
The first moment of an event is not on the stage.

2
00:00:04.535 --> 00:00:05.908
It is at registration.

3
00:00:05.978 --> 00:00:06.854
A queue.

4
00:00:06.924 --> 00:00:07.866
A question.

5
00:00:07.936 --> 00:00:09.750
A badge that needs to be right.

6
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.363
RegFire starts with that moment.

7
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:17.176
RegFire is shaped by Mike Blazejewski’s twenty-six years in registration and

8
00:00:17.246 --> 00:00:20.412
experience across thousands of onsite shows.

9
00:00:20.482 --> 00:00:21.673
Different audiences.

10
00:00:21.743 --> 00:00:22.237
Different rules.

11
00:00:22.307 --> 00:00:26.452
The same responsibility to make the opening moments work.

12
00:00:26.522 --> 00:00:31.314
That experience informs the product: practical control for organizers, and a

13
00:00:31.384 --> 00:00:34.119
clearer experience for the people arriving.

14
00:00:34.189 --> 00:00:37.836
In twenty twenty-five, business events brought together one point six five

15
00:00:37.906 --> 00:00:42.183
billion participants worldwide and generated one point three trillion dollars

16
00:00:42.253 --> 00:00:46.282
in direct spending, according to the Events Industry Council and Oxford

17
00:00:46.352 --> 00:00:47.560
Economics.

18
00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:52.123
Those are industry activity figures, not registration software revenue.

19
00:00:52.193 --> 00:00:56.355
RegFire’s opportunity is to earn a place in the workflows behind those

20
00:00:56.425 --> 00:00:57.716
experiences.

21
00:00:57.786 --> 00:01:02.262
A registration system has to reflect the event: who can attend, what they need

22
00:01:02.332 --> 00:01:05.946
to answer, which price applies, and what happens when they arrive.

23
00:01:06.016 --> 00:01:10.709
When those decisions are scattered across tools and workarounds, the organizer

24
00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:12.385
carries the complexity.

25
00:01:12.455 --> 00:01:17.878
RegFire’s thesis is simple: put that control into a configurable workspace.

26
00:01:17.948 --> 00:01:21.595
The current local product already demonstrates event setup, multiple

27
00:01:21.665 --> 00:01:26.656
registration flows, configurable questions, branding, pricing rules and

28
00:01:26.726 --> 00:01:28.100
attendee previews.

29
00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.800
Organizers can shape the journey and inspect it as they work.

30
00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:35.882
The footage here shows the actual application with demonstration data.

31
00:01:35.952 --> 00:01:40.280
It is a working foundation, not evidence of a completed commercial checkout or

32
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.155
live admission system.

33
00:01:42.225 --> 00:01:45.997
The initial commercial launch is planned to serve both ticketed events and

34
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:47.598
badge-based registration.

35
00:01:47.668 --> 00:01:51.133
That means completing the journey from registration and payment through

36
00:01:51.203 --> 00:01:53.356
credential delivery and arrival.

37
00:01:53.426 --> 00:01:58.550
Tickets, named badges, validation, corrections and reconciliation must work

38
00:01:58.620 --> 00:01:59.363
together.

39
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:01.736
Both paths are launch requirements.

40
00:02:01.806 --> 00:02:06.665
They still need end-to-end implementation, testing and operational proof.

41
00:02:06.735 --> 00:02:10.515
The integration vision is to connect registration with the systems organizers

42
00:02:10.585 --> 00:02:15.875
depend on: payment providers, membership records, customer systems and onsite

43
00:02:15.945 --> 00:02:16.854
equipment.

44
00:02:16.924 --> 00:02:20.637
Each connection needs a supported contract and a tested workflow.

45
00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:24.753
The aim is flexible integration, with clear boundaries, rather than a promise

46
00:02:24.823 --> 00:02:27.839
that every system already works with RegFire.

47
00:02:27.909 --> 00:02:31.921
AI assistance is part of the roadmap across the tools: helping an organizer

48
00:02:31.991 --> 00:02:36.601
configure an event, understand a feature, interpret their data and decide what

49
00:02:36.671 --> 00:02:37.613
to do next.

50
00:02:37.683 --> 00:02:40.866
The current Spark chat adapter is an early foundation.

51
00:02:40.936 --> 00:02:45.977
Useful assistance still requires grounded answers, permissions, evaluation and

52
00:02:46.047 --> 00:02:47.271
human control.

53
00:02:47.341 --> 00:02:52.291
AI throughout the workflow is the ambition, not a completed capability today.

54
00:02:52.361 --> 00:02:57.244
Onsite, the roadmap includes developing and customizing AI systems that can

55
00:02:57.314 --> 00:02:59.650
run locally, close to the operation.

56
00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:04.197
The goal is assistance tailored to the tasks, terminology and constraints of a

57
00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:07.997
live event, with less dependence on venue connectivity for supported

58
00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:08.993
functions.

59
00:03:09.063 --> 00:03:13.822
Model selection, adaptation, privacy and device performance still have to be

60
00:03:13.892 --> 00:03:14.535
proven.

61
00:03:14.605 --> 00:03:18.087
This is not a claim that RegFire has trained its own foundation model.

62
00:03:18.157 --> 00:03:22.501
The ambition spans small gatherings through major trade shows.

63
00:03:22.571 --> 00:03:26.882
The path starts with a defined operating envelope: supported event sizes,

64
00:03:26.952 --> 00:03:30.989
devices and workflows, then expands through measured performance.

65
00:03:31.059 --> 00:03:35.527
Large-show scale must be earned through load tests, rehearsal and real

66
00:03:35.597 --> 00:03:37.270
operational evidence.

67
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.190
It is a direction for the business, not a deployment claim.

68
00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:44.754
The commercial strategy is to serve repeat organizers with software they can

69
00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:49.151
configure themselves, supported by assisted setup and onsite services where

70
00:03:49.221 --> 00:03:49.931
needed.

71
00:03:50.001 --> 00:03:53.665
Pricing, retention and delivery economics must be tested with paying

72
00:03:53.735 --> 00:03:54.843
customers.

73
00:03:54.913 --> 00:03:58.992
The next milestone is a complete, dependable ticketing and badging workflow,

74
00:03:59.062 --> 00:04:02.095
followed by pilots that establish repeatable value.

75
00:04:02.165 --> 00:04:05.994
RegFire brings a practical point of view to an essential moment in every

76
00:04:06.064 --> 00:04:07.007
event.

77
00:04:07.077 --> 00:04:10.707
The opportunity is to turn that experience into dependable software,

78
00:04:10.777 --> 00:04:14.706
intelligent assistance and a business organizers return to.

79
00:04:14.776 --> 00:04:17.000
Let’s build the next chapter of registration.
